# D319, the pentest arc Tier 2 half of this corpus (testFlow M130c, PLAN_M130_PENTEST_TIER2.md) —
# `expect response has no authorization violations`, demonstrated firing and staying silent against
# the same app, one rule at a time.
#
# **The owner is `peer`, not `shopper`, and until `M137b` that was forced rather than chosen.** Tier 2
# judges a request that was actually made, so every test here needs a resource its owner really owns —
# and the seed creates users but no orders, so each test places one. `POST /orders` is a mutating
# request, and `shopper` is a cookie session: `AnyAuthGuard` refused it for a missing `X-CSRF-Token`
# before any of this got started. `peer` is bearer, so it could place the order it then asks about.
#
# **`M137b` removes that constraint and the arrangement stays anyway**, which is worth stating so the
# next reader does not take the paragraph above as still-binding. `shopper` now declares `csrf from`
# and could own every test in this file. It does not, because the inversion turned out to be a gift
# rather than a cost: it puts cookie-borne principals in the **probe set** instead of the owner slot,
# which is where they are actually interesting (`M130-01`). `csrf.tflw` is where a clause-declaring
# owner is exercised, because `sec/csrf-not-enforced` has nothing to derive from a bearer one.
#
# The probe set for every test in this file is therefore
# `{shopper, shopperBearer, shopperNoCsrf, anonymous}` — `admin` is declared `privileged` and
# excluded, and each assertion says so on its own line. The per-site cost is measured and printed by
# `verify-security-acceptance.mjs` rather than written down here, which is what D319 asks this corpus
# to do.
#
# **`shopperNoCsrf` is `M137b`'s addition to that set and it inherits `shopper`'s old job** (`D455`).
# The mutating rows below need a principal that *cannot* meet the CSRF pre-flight, so that a refusal
# reported as *inconclusive* rather than as a boundary that held stays observable after the milestone
# that removed the engine's reason for it. It is the same human on the same transport as `shopper`,
# declared without the clause.
#
# **`shopperBearer` is M132b's addition and it is what makes the mutating tests mean anything.**
# Before it, every principal in the probe set was excluded from a mutating verb for a correct
# reason — `shopper` cookie-borne and therefore inconclusive, `anonymous` refused `401`, `admin`
# privileged — and the exclusions composed until the set had nobody left able to answer. Alice
# declared a second time with a bearer token breaks that without inventing a new identity, and the
# `V8`/`V9` pair below is only a controlled comparison because she is there.
#
# `M137b` gives `shopper` a second way to answer and does **not** retire that reasoning: `D356`'s
# rejected alternative was to drop `shopperBearer` once `shopper` could answer, and it loses because
# the pair is this corpus's sharpest demonstration that a probe outcome belongs to the *credential*.
# With `shopperNoCsrf` also declared, the three alices now make that point in three states rather than
# two, and the two that can answer agree for a reason instead of differing by an accident of
# enforcement.
#
# **What the third state is doing somewhere else.** "Not applicable" cannot be written as a passing
# test: an assertion where nothing applied *fails*, by D285, and that is the whole point of it. So it
# lives in `scripts/verify-security-acceptance.mjs` as an expected-to-fail probe, the same place and
# for the same reason M128c put Tier 1's. D311's *default* half lives there too, for the same reason.
#
# Run under `--env secureLocal` (default), with `VULN_MODE=1` — the `vuln/` routes below do not
# exist without it, and their absence would read as a rule that stopped firing.

# **Every test gets its own product, and that is a bug this file already paid for.** The first draft
# ordered `GET /products` + `body[0].id`, which is whatever currently sorts first in a ~120-product
# catalog — and after a few runs against a long-lived stack that product's stock ran out and every
# test here failed on `409 insufficient stock`, nowhere near the authorization assertion they exist
# to make. `tests/api/identity/authz.tflw` carries the same fix and the same comment (M15); the
# lesson is that a corpus which accumulates state is a corpus that grades a different app each run.
#
# A bare `before` — not `before file` — because this shares each test's scope, including its
# `as peer`, so the order below is genuinely owned by the principal the assertion names.
before
  api POST /auth/login body { email: env(ADMIN_EMAIL), password: env(ADMIN_PW) }
  expect status equals 200
  capture body.accessToken as adminToken

  api GET /categories
  expect status equals 200
  capture body[0].id as categoryId

  # Its own stock, so nothing else in the catalog can starve it.
  api POST /products body { name: unique("Authz Acceptance Widget"), price: 10, stock: 10, categoryId: {categoryId} }
    header "Authorization" is "Bearer {adminToken}"
  expect status equals 201
  capture body.id as productId

  # Placed as `peer`, because a bare `before` runs inside the test's own scope.
  api POST /orders body { items: [{ productId: "{productId}", quantity: 1 }] }
  expect status equals 201
  capture body.id as orderId

# ── the two positives ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

@authz @vuln
test "sec/authz-object-leak fires on a route that serves any order by id (V6)" as peer
  # The clean counterpart is `GET /orders/{orderId}`, two tests down. Both answer `200` with the same
  # body to their owner; the only difference is who *else* gets it, which is exactly the difference a
  # status-code oracle cannot see.
  api GET /vuln/orders/{orderId}
  expect status equals 200
  # `shopper` re-issues this and gets `peer`'s order back — same root `id`, so the identity the
  # comparison is on is shared, and that is the finding. So do her other two credentials: `GET` is a
  # safe method, so nothing here turns on CSRF and all three alices leak identically, which is the
  # correct answer three times rather than three findings. `anonymous` is refused `401` by
  # `AnyAuthGuard`, which is correct and counted rather than ignored.
  expect response not has no critical authorization violations

@authz @vuln
test "sec/authz-collection-leak fires on a route that returns every user's orders (V7)" as peer
  api GET /vuln/orders
  expect status equals 200
  # A `200` carrying a JSON array is what the clean `GET /orders` returns too. The rule compares the
  # root ids in the owner's array against the ones each probe received; here they overlap completely,
  # because this route filters by nothing at all.
  expect response not has no critical authorization violations

# ── the same two rules, applicable and silent ────────────────────────────────────────────────────

@authz
test "sec/authz-object-leak stays silent on the real ownership-scoped route" as peer
  # Applicable, and correct. `findOneScoped` admits the owner, an admin, and an owner/admin of the
  # placing user's org, and throws `ForbiddenException('not your order')` for anyone else — so
  # `shopper` gets `403` and the boundary is *demonstrated*, not merely un-violated.
  api GET /orders/{orderId}
  expect status equals 200
  expect response has no authorization violations

@authz
test "sec/authz-collection-leak stays silent on the real order list" as peer
  # `findOwn(user.id)` scopes to the caller, so `shopper` gets her own list and none of `peer`'s ids
  # appear in it. The rule ran on a response that genuinely meets its precondition — an array of
  # objects with root ids — which is what separates "silent" from "never engaged".
  #
  # Measured outcome: `1 served different content, 1 refused`. This is the only place in either
  # corpus where D313's **served-different** state occurs live, and it is the state most easily
  # mistaken for a leak — `shopper` got a `200` carrying orders, just not `peer`'s. A status oracle
  # sees two identical `200`s here and on `/vuln/orders`; only the identity comparison separates them.
  api GET /orders
  expect status equals 200
  expect response has no authorization violations

# ── D311, the opt-in half: this env declares `probe mutating` ────────────────────────────────────

@authz @vuln @mutating
test "probe mutating puts a DELETE in the probe set, and a replay cannot judge it (V8)" as peer
  # `DELETE` is not probed unless the `authorized target` for this host says `probe mutating`, and
  # this env's does. The default half — the same request under `--env plaintext`, whose target makes
  # no such declaration — is graded in `verify-security-acceptance.mjs`, because an assertion whose
  # whole probe set was declined has no power to fail and therefore cannot be a passing test.
  #
  # **This assertion is `has no`, not `not has no`, and that is the milestone's own correction.**
  # D319 predicted the opt-in "probes it and finds the leak". It does the first and not the second,
  # and the measured reason is structural rather than a bug: this route really is exploitable —
  # `verify-security-target.mjs` deletes an order as a non-owner and proves it — but a *replay*
  # oracle cannot see it. The owner's own `DELETE` succeeds first and destroys the row, so every
  # probe that follows asks about a resource that no longer exists. The bound is destruction, not
  # mutation: an idempotent `PUT`/`PATCH` would leak normally. See `M130-05`.
  #
  # So what this test pins is the half that is real: `probe mutating` moved the request from
  # `not probed` into the probe set, and the run says what each principal did there —
  # `shopperNoCsrf` **inconclusive** (cookie-borne with no `csrf from` clause, refused `403` on a
  # mutating verb: CSRF, not authorization — `M130-01`'s path, exercised) and everyone else
  # **refused**, because the row is already gone. One judgeable outcome is enough for applicability,
  # so this is a real verdict rather than a skipped one, and the verdict is honest about a probe set
  # that had nobody left able to answer.
  #
  # Before `M137b` the inconclusive principal here was `shopper` itself. It now supplies the token and
  # joins the refusals; `shopperNoCsrf` was declared so that this line still has a subject (`D455`).
  api DELETE /vuln/orders/{orderId}
  expect status equals 200
  expect response has no authorization violations

@authz @vuln @mutating
test "probe mutating finds the leak on an idempotent PUT, which a DELETE cannot show (V9)" as peer
  # **The positive `probe mutating` shipped without, and the reason `M130-05` stayed open through
  # two milestones.** The opt-in's only mutating plant was the `DELETE` above, whose verdict is out
  # of reach of a replay oracle, so the control's evidence was "the request was probed" and never
  # "the leak was found" — D291's vacuous shape wearing a new costume.
  #
  # **Read this test and the one above it as a pair; individually neither proves the point.** Same
  # host, same `probe mutating` declaration, same probe set, same owner, orders placed the same way
  # by the same `before`. The single variable is whether the verb destroys what it touches. The
  # `DELETE` comes back with no verdict and the `PUT` comes back a critical, so "a replay cannot
  # judge destruction" is now shown by contrast rather than argued — and, just as importantly, the
  # competing explanation is ruled out: it is *not* that nobody in the probe set could answer,
  # because on this request `shopperBearer` answers.
  #
  # A new order rather than `{orderId}`: the `DELETE` above already destroyed that one, and asking
  # about a row that no longer exists would produce a `404` and an assertion with no power to fail
  # — the failure mode this corpus exists to make visible, reached by accident.
  api POST /orders body { items: [{ productId: "{productId}", quantity: 1 }] }
  expect status equals 201
  capture body.id as putOrderId

  # Idempotent: `status` is written to the value it is given, so the probes' replays leave the row
  # exactly where the owner's request left it and each probe asks the same question of the same
  # state. `shopperNoCsrf` is refused for CSRF and reported **inconclusive** — cookie-borne with no
  # clause, on a mutating verb, which is `M130-01`'s path and the reason a cookie principal is not the
  # interesting owner here. `shopperBearer` is the same human with a bearer token, is refused nothing,
  # and gets `peer`'s order back by id: that is the leak. **After `M137b` so does `shopper`** — same
  # human, cookie transport, token supplied by the clause — so this row carries two leaks and they are
  # the same leak reached by two credentials. `anonymous` is `401` and refused.
  api PUT /vuln/orders/{putOrderId} body { status: "processing" }
  expect status equals 200
  expect response not has no critical authorization violations
